{"kind":"artifact","text":".github/scripts at exact clean pushed head 01d602d5b4241cb6dc0c245aa1cdb3b54df08a39 (branch request/repo-security-gates).\n\nA repository-owned secret scanner and its own test. Generic entropy scanners flag this history by the hundred - event identifiers, commit object ids, SSH public keys, signature material - and a gate that is wrong most of the time is one people learn to click past, so this matches issuer formats and scans the tracked current tree. Both halves of that scope are pinned: untracked files are ignored, tracked ones are scanned. The test proves five secret shapes are detected, three repository-native shapes are not, and this repository passes its own gate.\n\nAnswering child request be158c2e under promise 15999b98, which narrowed parent 6ace1fbe after my assert cba54293 showed its SECURITY.md condition contradicted ratified decision 27ad16c2.\n\nGates at this exact clean pushed head: go test ./... 30 packages ok, go vet clean, make docs clean, make ui-check clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, branch on origin. Note that ui-check first failed with 'tsc: command not found', which is a fresh worktree missing node_modules rather than a code defect; it passes after npm ci.\n\nTwo of codex's added constraints caught real defects in what I had already built, and I found neither on my own.\n\nThe scanner's own test file contained literal token shapes, so once tracked the scanner flagged five candidates in it and exited 1. I had run the scanner before committing the test, so I validated a tree that did not yet contain its own fixtures - the gate would have failed on its own evidence the first time CI ran it. Fixtures are now assembled at runtime from parts, and a case asserts this repository passes its own scanner with the test file tracked.\n\nThe rm -rf constraint applied to my test cleanup, which used trap 'rm -rf' RETURN. It now removes only what the test created, without a recursive force delete.\n\nDeliberately not done, and flagged rather than decided: .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh line 10 already contains rm -rf, predating this work. The constraint would cover it, but changing an unrelated existing gate inside a security diff is scope creep, so I asked rather than acting.\n\nOn validating YAML semantics: I tried declaring gopkg.in/yaml.v3 and reverted it. It pulls three further modules into go.sum, and widening the dependency graph inside a change meant to narrow supply-chain exposure is the wrong trade. internal/ciguard parses the fixed structure directly instead - strict enough that an ecosystem named only in a comment, a wrong directory, or a missing interval each fail. The tradeoff is stated in the code so a reviewer can overrule it; the dependency is one line.\n\nSECURITY.md is untouched and the public_surface_test gate stands.","body":{"commit":"01d602d5b4241cb6dc0c245aa1cdb3b54df08a39","path":".github/scripts"}}